/* Past-paper practice: Section 1 multiple choice. Original questions in the style of recent papers (not copied). */
(function () {
  "use strict";
  const B = (window.HPP_BANK = window.HPP_BANK || {});
  B.s1 = {
    intro: "Section 1 of the paper is <strong>20 marks of multiple choice</strong>, one mark each, with five options (A to E) and a single correct answer. This bank gives you more than twice that many: a spread of recall and concept items, one- and two-step calculations, and questions built on a graph or diagram, across Our Dynamic Universe, Particles and Waves, Electricity and the practical skills. Most wrong options are the classic slips (a missing square, a forgotten unit conversion, the wrong way round), so work the answer out <em>before</em> looking at the options, then check which option matches and why the others do not.",
    questions: [
      {"id":"s1-01","style":"Motion graphs · displacement from a v–t graph","stem":"<p>A trolley moves along a straight horizontal track. The graph shows how its velocity changes during a 12 s journey.</p><p>What is the displacement of the trolley during the 12 s?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Velocity-time graph for a trolley: velocity rises in a straight line from 0 to 8 metres per second between 0 and 4 seconds, stays at 8 metres per second until 10 seconds, then falls in a straight line to 0 at 12 seconds.\" viewBox=\"0 0 380 270\" width=\"380\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"16\" width=\"304\" height=\"206\" fill=\"#fbfcfe\" stroke=\"#ccd\"/><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"58\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"108.7\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"108.7\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"108.7\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"108.7\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"108.7\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >2</text><line x1=\"159.3\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"159.3\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"159.3\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"159.3\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"159.3\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >4</text><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >6</text><line x1=\"260.7\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"260.7\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"260.7\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"260.7\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"260.7\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >8</text><line x1=\"311.3\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"311.3\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"311.3\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"311.3\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"311.3\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >10</text><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"362\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >12</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"180.8\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"180.8\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"180.8\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"180.8\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"184.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >2</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"139.6\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"139.6\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"139.6\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"139.6\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"143.6\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >4</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"98.4\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"98.4\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"98.4\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"98.4\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"102.4\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >6</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"57.2\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"57.2\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"57.2\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"57.2\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"61.2\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >8</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >10</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >t / s</text><text transform=\"translate(14 119) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\">v / m s⁻¹</text><polyline points=\"58,222 159.3,57.2 311.3,57.2 362,222\" fill=\"#e3ecf8\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["8.0 m","48 m","72 m","64 m","96 m"],"a":2,"why":"Displacement is the <strong>area under the v–t graph</strong>: ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m, plus 6 × 8 = 48 m, plus ½ × 2 × 8 = 8 m, giving <strong>72 m</strong> (equivalently, a trapezium: ½ × (12 + 6) × 8). 96 m assumes the trolley moves at 8 m s⁻¹ for the whole 12 s; 64 m leaves out the final triangle; 48 m counts only the constant-velocity section; 8.0 m is just the greatest velocity read from the axis."}]},
      {"id":"s1-02","style":"Projectile · horizontal launch","stem":"<p>A stone is thrown horizontally at 12 m s⁻¹ from the top of a vertical cliff 19.6 m high. Air resistance is negligible.</p><p>How far from the foot of the cliff does the stone land?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["24 m","12 m","17 m","48 m","235 m"],"a":0,"why":"Treat the motion in two parts. Vertically, u = 0 so s = ½at²: 19.6 = ½ × 9.8 × t², giving t = 2.0 s. Horizontally the velocity is constant, so d = vt = 12 × 2.0 = <strong>24 m</strong>. 17 m comes from leaving out the ½ in s = ½at² (t = 1.4 s); 48 m doubles the fall time; 12 m is the speed, not a distance; 235 m multiplies the speed by the height."}]},
      {"id":"s1-03","style":"Vertical motion · maximum height","stem":"<p>A ball is thrown vertically upwards with an initial speed of 14.7 m s⁻¹. Air resistance is negligible.</p><p>What is the maximum height reached by the ball above the point of release?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["7.4 m","1.5 m","22 m","11 m","26 m"],"a":3,"why":"At the top the ball is momentarily at rest, so v² = u² + 2as gives 0 = 14.7² + 2 × (−9.8) × s, so s = 14.7² ÷ 19.6 = <strong>11 m</strong> (11 m to 2 s.f.). 22 m leaves out the 2 (it is v²/g); 1.5 m is the <em>time</em> to the top v/g, not the height; 7.4 m is half the speed (14.7 ÷ 2); 26 m adds the speed to the correct height (11 + 14.7), which mixes quantities with different units."}]},
      {"id":"s1-04","style":"Newton's second law · resultant force","stem":"<p>A van of mass 1200 kg is driven along a level road. The engine produces a forward force of 3000 N and the total resistive force on the van is 600 N.</p><p>What is the acceleration of the van?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.50 m s⁻²","2.5 m s⁻²","3.0 m s⁻²","5.0 m s⁻²","2.0 m s⁻²"],"a":4,"why":"The unbalanced force is 3000 − 600 = 2400 N, so a = F/m = 2400 ÷ 1200 = <strong>2.0 m s⁻²</strong>. 2.5 m s⁻² ignores the resistive force; 3.0 m s⁻² adds it instead of subtracting it; 0.50 m s⁻² uses the resistive force alone; 5.0 m s⁻² divides the driving force by the resistive force. Only the <em>resultant</em> force is used in F = ma."}]},
      {"id":"s1-05","style":"Forces · apparent weight in a lift","stem":"<p>A person of mass 60 kg stands on a weighing platform in a lift. The lift accelerates <strong>upwards</strong> at 1.5 m s⁻².</p><p>What is the force exerted by the platform on the person?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["498 N","678 N","588 N","90 N","882 N"],"a":1,"why":"Take upward as positive: R − mg = ma, so R = m(g + a) = 60 × (9.8 + 1.5) = <strong>678 N</strong>. 588 N is the weight, which is the platform force only when the lift is not accelerating; 498 N subtracts the acceleration (the lift would be accelerating downwards); 90 N is only the resultant force; 882 N multiplies g by a."}]},
      {"id":"s1-06","style":"Collisions · conservation of momentum","stem":"<p>A trolley of mass 0.80 kg moving at 1.5 m s⁻¹ collides with a stationary trolley of mass 0.40 kg. The trolleys stick together and move off in a straight line. Friction is negligible.</p><p>What is the speed of the trolleys immediately after the collision?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.50 m s⁻¹","0.75 m s⁻¹","1.0 m s⁻¹","1.5 m s⁻¹","2.3 m s⁻¹"],"a":2,"why":"Total momentum is conserved: 0.80 × 1.5 + 0 = (0.80 + 0.40) × v, so v = 1.2 ÷ 1.2 = <strong>1.0 m s⁻¹</strong>. 1.5 m s⁻¹ forgets that the mass has increased; 0.75 m s⁻¹ divides by 1.6 as if the moving trolley's mass counted twice; 0.50 m s⁻¹ uses only the 0.40 kg mass (0.40 × 1.5 ÷ 1.2); 2.3 m s⁻¹ multiplies the speeds (1.5 × 1.5)."}]},
      {"id":"s1-07","style":"Impulse · area under a force–time graph","stem":"<p>A bat strikes a stationary ball of mass 0.50 kg. The graph shows how the force of the bat on the ball varies with time during the contact.</p><p>What is the speed of the ball just after it leaves the bat?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Force-time graph for a bat striking a ball: the force rises in a straight line from 0 newtons at 0 milliseconds to a peak of 400 newtons at 10 milliseconds, then falls in a straight line back to 0 newtons at 20 milliseconds.\" viewBox=\"0 0 380 270\" width=\"380\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"16\" width=\"304\" height=\"206\" fill=\"#fbfcfe\" stroke=\"#ccd\"/><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"58\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"134\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"134\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"134\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >5</text><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >10</text><line x1=\"286\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"286\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"286\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"286\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"286\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >15</text><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"362\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >20</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"180.8\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"180.8\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"180.8\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"180.8\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"184.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >100</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"139.6\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"139.6\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"139.6\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"139.6\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"143.6\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >200</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"98.4\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"98.4\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"98.4\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"98.4\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"102.4\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >300</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"57.2\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"57.2\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"57.2\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"57.2\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"61.2\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >400</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >500</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >t / ms</text><text transform=\"translate(14 119) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\">F / N</text><polyline points=\"58,222 210,57.2 362,222\" fill=\"#e3ecf8\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"57.2\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["4 m s⁻¹","16 m s⁻¹","800 m s⁻¹","2.0 m s⁻¹","8 m s⁻¹"],"a":4,"why":"The impulse is the area under the graph: ½ × 0.020 s × 400 N = 4.0 N s. Then Ft = mv − mu gives 4.0 = 0.50 × v, so v = <strong>8 m s⁻¹</strong>. 4.0 is the impulse itself (units N s, not m s⁻¹); 16 m s⁻¹ takes the area as a rectangle, 400 × 0.020; 800 m s⁻¹ divides the peak force by the mass; 2.0 m s⁻¹ multiplies the impulse by the mass instead of dividing by it."}]},
      {"id":"s1-08","style":"Energy · work done against friction","stem":"<p>A skier of mass 60 kg starts from rest and descends a slope, falling through a vertical height of 30 m. At the bottom the skier’s speed is 20 m s⁻¹.</p><p>How much energy is transformed to heat by friction and air resistance on the way down?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.2 × 10⁴ J","1.76 × 10⁴ J","2.96 × 10⁴ J","5.6 × 10³ J","6.0 × 10³ J"],"a":3,"why":"Energy lost = loss of gravitational potential energy − gain in kinetic energy = mgh − ½mv² = 17640 − 12000 = <strong>5640 J</strong>. 12000 J is just the final kinetic energy; 17640 J is just the potential energy lost; 29640 J adds the two energies; 6.0 × 10³ J is half of E<sub>k</sub> and has no physical meaning here."}]},
      {"id":"s1-09","style":"Work, energy and power · power of a crane","stem":"<p>A crane lifts a 500 kg load vertically through 12 m at constant speed in 20 s.</p><p>What is the useful output power of the crane?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["300 W","2.94 × 10³ W","5.88 × 10⁴ W","1.2 × 10⁶ W","8.2 × 10³ W"],"a":1,"why":"Work done = force × distance = mgh = 500 × 9.8 × 12 = 58800 J. Power = E/t = 58800 ÷ 20 = <strong>2940 W</strong>. 300 W forgets g; 5.88 × 10⁴ W is the energy, not the power (no division by time); 1.2 × 10⁶ W multiplies the energy by the time instead of dividing; 8.2 × 10³ W swaps the distance and time (mg × 20 ÷ 12)."}]},
      {"id":"s1-10","style":"Gravitation · Newton's law","stem":"<p>Two small spheres, of mass 5.0 kg and 8.0 kg, are placed with their centres 0.50 m apart in deep space.</p><p>What is the gravitational force between them?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.1 × 10⁻⁸ N","5.3 × 10⁻⁹ N","3.5 × 10⁻⁹ N","2.7 × 10⁻⁹ N","4.3 × 10⁻⁸ N"],"a":0,"why":"F = Gm₁m₂/r² = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 5.0 × 8.0 ÷ 0.50² = <strong>1.1 × 10⁻⁸ N</strong>. The distractors are the classic slips: r not squared (5.3 × 10⁻⁹ N); adding the masses instead of multiplying (3.5 × 10⁻⁹ N); using r = 1.0 m (2.7 × 10⁻⁹ N); and squaring 0.25 m instead of 0.50 m (4.3 × 10⁻⁸ N)."}]},
      {"id":"s1-11","style":"Special relativity · time dilation","stem":"<p>A spacecraft travels past the Earth at 0.80<em>c</em>. A clock on the spacecraft records an interval of 12 s between two events that happen at the same place on board.</p><p>What time interval is measured between the same two events by an observer on Earth?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["7.2 s","9.6 s","12 s","20 s","15 s"],"a":3,"why":"The 12 s is the proper time (both events are at the same place on the spacecraft), so the Earth observer measures a <em>longer</em> time: t′ = t/√(1 − (v/c)²) = 12 ÷ √(1 − 0.80²) = 12 ÷ 0.60 = <strong>20 s</strong>. 7.2 s and 9.6 s multiply instead of divide (a ‘shorter’ time is wrong); 15 s divides by 0.80 instead of by √(1 − 0.64)."}]},
      {"id":"s1-12","style":"Special relativity · length contraction","stem":"<p>A space station has a length of 80 m in its own rest frame. A spacecraft flies past it, parallel to its length, at 0.60<em>c</em>.</p><p>What is the length of the station measured by an observer on the spacecraft?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["48 m","64 m","51 m","80 m","100 m"],"a":1,"why":"Moving lengths are contracted: l′ = l√(1 − (v/c)²) = 80 × √(1 − 0.60²) = 80 × 0.80 = <strong>64 m</strong>. 100 m divides by 0.80 (a lengthening); 48 m multiplies by v/c = 0.60 instead of by 0.80; 51 m forgets the square root (80 × 0.64); 80 m ignores relativity."}]},
      {"id":"s1-13","style":"Doppler effect · moving source","stem":"<p>An ambulance siren emits a sound of frequency 800 Hz. The ambulance travels at 25 m s⁻¹ directly towards a stationary observer.</p><p>What frequency does the observer hear?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["863 Hz","745 Hz","775 Hz","800 Hz","825 Hz"],"a":0,"why":"For a source moving towards the observer the observed frequency is higher: f<sub>o</sub> = f<sub>s</sub> × v/(v − v<sub>s</sub>) = 800 × 340 ÷ (340 − 25) = <strong>863 Hz</strong>. 745 Hz is the frequency heard if the ambulance were moving <em>away</em> (v + v<sub>s</sub>); 775 Hz and 825 Hz subtract or add the speeds directly to the frequency, which is meaningless dimensionally; 800 Hz is the frequency heard only when there is no relative motion."}]},
      {"id":"s1-14","style":"Expanding Universe · Hubble–Lemaître law","stem":"<p>The light from a distant galaxy has a redshift <em>z</em> of 0.012. Assume this is due entirely to the expansion of the Universe.</p><p>What is the distance to the galaxy?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["8.3 × 10⁻¹² m","3.6 × 10⁶ m","1.3 × 10²⁶ m","5.2 × 10¹⁵ m","1.6 × 10²⁴ m"],"a":4,"why":"The recession speed is v = zc = 0.012 × 3.00 × 10⁸ = 3.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. Then v = H₀d gives d = v/H₀ = 3.6 × 10⁶ ÷ 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ = <strong>1.6 × 10²⁴ m</strong>. 8.3 × 10⁻¹² multiplies instead of dividing by H₀; 3.6 × 10⁶ is the recession speed; 1.3 × 10²⁶ uses v = c rather than v = zc; 5.2 × 10¹⁵ forgets to multiply z by c."}]},
      {"id":"s1-15","style":"Big Bang theory · evidence","stem":"<p>Which of the following is evidence that supports the Big Bang theory of the origin of the Universe?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The Sun’s spectrum contains dark absorption lines","The stars nearest the Sun show a small parallax shift over a year","A faint microwave background radiation detected from all directions in space","The planets orbit the Sun in ellipses","Light from most distant galaxies is shifted towards the blue end of the spectrum"],"a":2,"why":"The <strong>cosmic microwave background</strong> is the cooled-down remnant of radiation from the hot early Universe, and the observed abundance of hydrogen and helium and the redshift of distant galaxies also support the theory. Absorption lines tell us about the Sun’s atmosphere, parallax measures distances to near stars and planetary orbits come from gravity. Distant galaxies are <em>red</em>shifted, not blueshifted, which is what an expanding Universe predicts."}]},
      {"id":"s1-16","style":"Forces · terminal velocity","stem":"<p>A parachutist falls vertically with the parachute open and, after a time, reaches a constant speed called the terminal velocity.</p><p>Which statement about the forces on the parachutist at terminal velocity is correct?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The air resistance is equal in size to the weight, so the resultant force is zero","The air resistance is greater than the weight, so the parachutist is slowing down","The air resistance is zero, so the weight is the only force acting","The weight has become zero, so there is no resultant force","The air resistance is less than the weight but the parachutist moves at constant speed"],"a":0,"why":"At constant velocity Newton’s first law requires a <strong>zero resultant force</strong>: the upward air resistance balances the downward weight. If air resistance were larger the parachutist would be decelerating; if smaller, accelerating. Weight does not vanish and air resistance does not become zero – it grows with speed until it equals the weight."}]},
      {"id":"s1-17","style":"Explosions · conservation of momentum","stem":"<p>Two trolleys of masses 2.0 kg and 1.0 kg are at rest, in contact, on a level bench. A spring between them is released and the trolleys move apart in opposite directions. The 2.0 kg trolley moves off at 0.50 m s⁻¹. Friction is negligible.</p><p>What is the speed of the 1.0 kg trolley?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.25 m s⁻¹","0.50 m s⁻¹","1.5 m s⁻¹","1.0 m s⁻¹","2.0 m s⁻¹"],"a":3,"why":"The total momentum before is zero, so it is zero after: 2.0 × 0.50 = 1.0 × v, giving v = <strong>1.0 m s⁻¹</strong> in the opposite direction. 0.50 m s⁻¹ assumes equal speeds; 0.25 m s⁻¹ divides by the wrong mass; 1.5 m s⁻¹ adds the masses; 2.0 m s⁻¹ uses the mass of the other trolley as if it were a speed."}]},
      {"id":"s1-18","style":"Dark matter · galaxy rotation","stem":"<p>Stars in the outer parts of many spiral galaxies are observed to orbit the galactic centre faster than the visible matter in the galaxy can explain.</p><p>Which conclusion do astronomers draw from this evidence?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The galaxy is moving away from the Earth and is redshifted","The galaxy contains extra mass that does not emit light, called dark matter","The expansion of the Universe is slowing down","The outer stars are younger than the inner stars","The gravitational constant is larger at the edge of the galaxy"],"a":1,"why":"The orbital speed of a star depends on the mass inside its orbit through gravity. Faster-than-expected speeds mean there is <strong>more mass than we can see</strong> – dark matter. Redshift gives the galaxy’s recession speed, not its internal rotation; the evidence for dark energy is the accelerating expansion; and G is a universal constant."}]},
      {"id":"s1-19","style":"Standard Model · force carriers","stem":"<p>In beta-minus decay a down quark in a neutron changes into an up quark, and an electron and an antineutrino are emitted.</p><p>Which exchange particle is responsible for this change of quark flavour?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["Photon","Gluon","W⁻ boson","Graviton","Z⁰ boson"],"a":2,"why":"Changing the flavour of a quark (and its charge, from −⅓ to +⅔) is a <strong>weak interaction</strong> carried by W bosons: the down quark emits a W⁻, which becomes an electron and an antineutrino. The photon carries the electromagnetic force and gluons the strong force; the graviton (gravity) is far too weak to matter. The Z⁰ is also a weak-force carrier but is neutral, so it cannot carry away the unit of charge needed."}]},
      {"id":"s1-20","style":"Standard Model · hadrons","stem":"<p>A pion is a type of meson.</p><p>Which description of the structure of a meson is correct?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["Three quarks","Two quarks and one antiquark","One quark and one lepton","Two leptons","One quark and one antiquark"],"a":4,"why":"<strong>Hadrons</strong> are particles made of quarks. <em>Baryons</em> (protons, neutrons) are three quarks, while <em>mesons</em> are a quark–antiquark pair. Leptons (electrons, neutrinos) are fundamental and are not part of hadrons. Two quarks and one antiquark is not a hadron structure at all, and three quarks is a baryon, not a meson."}]},
      {"id":"s1-21","style":"Nuclear reactions · E = mc²","stem":"<p>In a nuclear reaction the total mass of the products is less than the total mass of the reactants by 3.6 × 10⁻²⁸ kg.</p><p>How much energy is released in this reaction?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.1 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","4.0 × 10⁻⁴⁵ J","1.6 × 10⁻¹¹ J","3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ J","2.9 × 10⁶ J"],"a":3,"why":"The energy released equals the mass lost multiplied by <em>c</em>²: E = mc² = 3.6 × 10⁻²⁸ × (3.00 × 10⁸)² = <strong>3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ J</strong>. Using <em>c</em> instead of <em>c</em>² gives 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; using c⁴ gives 2.9 × 10⁶ J; dividing by <em>c</em>² gives 4.0 × 10⁻⁴⁵ J; and ½mc² (1.6 × 10⁻¹¹ J) mixes up the formula with kinetic energy."}]},
      {"id":"s1-22","style":"Nuclear reactions · fusion","stem":"<p>Fusion of light nuclei releases energy, but it only occurs at extremely high temperatures, such as inside a star.</p><p>Why is a very high temperature required?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["Heavy nuclei must be split into smaller pieces, which needs a lot of energy","The neutrons must be slowed down so that they are captured by the nuclei","The nuclei must have enough kinetic energy to overcome their electrostatic repulsion and get close enough for the strong force to bind them","The electrons must be removed from the nuclei so that they become radioactive","The temperature must be high enough to increase the mass of the nuclei"],"a":2,"why":"All nuclei are positively charged and repel each other. They only fuse if they approach to within the very short range of the <strong>strong force</strong>, which needs a high kinetic energy – hence high temperature (or huge pressure). Splitting heavy nuclei describes fission, slowing neutrons is a fission-reactor moderator, and temperature does not change the number of nucleons."}]},
      {"id":"s1-23","style":"Photoelectric effect · maximum kinetic energy","stem":"<p>The threshold frequency of a metal is 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Light of frequency 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz is shone on the metal surface.</p><p>What is the maximum kinetic energy of a photoelectron emitted from the surface?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["5.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","8.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","9.9 × 10⁻²⁰ J","2.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"],"a":4,"why":"E<sub>k</sub> = hf − hf₀ = h(f − f₀) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × (8.0 − 5.0) × 10¹⁴ = <strong>2.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</strong>. 5.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is the whole photon energy; 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is the work function; 8.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J adds the frequencies; the half-value has no basis in the equation."}]},
      {"id":"s1-24","style":"Photoelectric effect · interpreting a graph","stem":"<p>A student measures the maximum kinetic energy E<sub>k</sub> of photoelectrons emitted from a metal for several frequencies <em>f</em> of light, and plots the graph shown.</p><p>What does the gradient of the line represent?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Graph of maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons against frequency of the incident light for one metal: a straight line starting at zero kinetic energy at a frequency of 4.0 times ten to the fourteen hertz and rising to 4.0 times ten to the minus nineteen joules at a frequency of 10 times ten to the fourteen hertz.\" viewBox=\"0 0 380 270\" width=\"380\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"16\" width=\"304\" height=\"206\" fill=\"#fbfcfe\" stroke=\"#ccd\"/><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"58\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"118.8\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"118.8\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"118.8\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"118.8\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"118.8\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >2</text><line x1=\"179.6\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"179.6\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"179.6\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"179.6\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"179.6\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >4</text><line x1=\"240.4\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"240.4\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"240.4\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"240.4\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"240.4\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >6</text><line x1=\"301.2\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"301.2\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"301.2\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"301.2\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"301.2\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >8</text><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"362\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >10</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"170.5\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"170.5\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"170.5\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"170.5\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"174.5\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >1</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"119\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"119\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"119\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"119\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >2</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"67.5\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"67.5\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"67.5\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"67.5\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"71.5\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >3</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >4</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >f / 10¹⁴ Hz</text><text transform=\"translate(14 119) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\">Eₖ / 10⁻¹⁹ J</text><line x1=\"179.6\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"17\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" /><circle cx=\"179.6\" cy=\"222\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The Planck constant, h","The threshold frequency of the metal","The work function of the metal","The charge on an electron","The speed of light"],"a":0,"why":"From E<sub>k</sub> = hf − hf₀, a graph of E<sub>k</sub> against <em>f</em> is a straight line of <strong>gradient h</strong> (the Planck constant, here (4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹) ÷ (6.0 × 10¹⁴) = about 6.7 × 10⁻³⁴ J s). The threshold frequency f₀ is the intercept on the <em>f</em> axis (4.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz); the work function hf₀ is minus the intercept on the E<sub>k</sub> axis when the line is extended back; e and c do not appear in the relationship."}]},
      {"id":"s1-25","style":"Photoelectric effect · irradiance","stem":"<p>Ultraviolet light of frequency above the threshold frequency causes photoelectrons to be emitted from a clean zinc plate. The irradiance of the light is then doubled, without changing its frequency.</p><p>Which row describes the effect on the photoelectrons?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The number of photoelectrons emitted per second is unchanged; the maximum kinetic energy doubles","The number of photoelectrons emitted per second doubles; the maximum kinetic energy is unchanged","The number of photoelectrons emitted per second doubles; the maximum kinetic energy doubles","The number of photoelectrons emitted per second is unchanged; the maximum kinetic energy is unchanged","No more photoelectrons are emitted, because the threshold frequency has doubled"],"a":1,"why":"Irradiance is the energy per second per unit area, so doubling it at fixed frequency means <strong>twice as many photons</strong> per second, each of the same energy hf. Each photon frees at most one electron, so twice as many electrons are emitted, but each photon still has energy hf, so the maximum E<sub>k</sub> = hf − hf₀ does not change. The threshold frequency belongs to the metal and cannot be altered by the light."}]},
      {"id":"s1-26","style":"Spectra · energy level transition","stem":"<p>The energy levels of an atom are shown. An electron makes the transition from E₃ to E₂, shown by the arrow, and a photon is emitted.</p><p>What is the frequency of the photon?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Energy level diagram for an atom showing four levels E4, E3, E2 and E1 at minus 1.0, minus 2.4, minus 5.4 and minus 8.7 times ten to the minus nineteen joules, with ionisation at zero. A downward arrow marks an electron transition from level E3 to level E2.\" viewBox=\"0 0 440 260\" width=\"440\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"62\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >E₄</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">−1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"82.8\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"82.8\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"62\" y=\"86.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >E₃</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"86.8\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">−2.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"148.8\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"148.8\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"62\" y=\"152.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >E₂</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"152.8\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">−5.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"221.4\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"221.4\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"62\" y=\"225.4\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >E₁</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"225.4\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">−8.7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"84.8\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"144.8\" stroke=\"#b3261e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"145,138.8 155,138.8 150,146.8\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.2 × 10¹⁵ Hz","8.1 × 10¹⁴ Hz","6.6 × 10¹⁴ Hz","3.6 × 10¹⁴ Hz","4.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz"],"a":4,"why":"The photon energy is the difference between the levels: ΔE = E₃ − E₂ = (−2.4) − (−5.4) = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Then f = ΔE/h = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = <strong>4.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz</strong>. 1.2 × 10¹⁵ Hz adds the two energies; 8.1 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 3.6 × 10¹⁴ Hz use a single level rather than the difference; 6.6 × 10¹⁴ Hz uses the E₄ → E₂ transition."}]},
      {"id":"s1-27","style":"Spectra · absorption lines","stem":"<p>The continuous spectrum from the hot interior of a star is crossed by dark lines when it is viewed from the Earth.</p><p>What causes the dark lines?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["Atoms in the outer atmosphere emit photons of those frequencies only, which cancel the continuous spectrum","The star is moving away from the Earth, which shifts those frequencies out of the visible region","Atoms in the cooler outer atmosphere absorb photons with energies equal to the gaps between their energy levels","The Earth’s atmosphere reflects those frequencies back into space","Electrons in the interior of the star are not excited enough to emit those frequencies"],"a":2,"why":"Photons whose energy hf matches the energy difference between two levels in an atom can be <strong>absorbed</strong> by the cooler gas, exciting its electrons; these frequencies are then missing from the light that continues to us, leaving dark lines at the same positions as that element’s emission lines. Redshift shifts the whole pattern of lines, it does not create them."}]},
      {"id":"s1-28","style":"Diffraction grating · second order maximum","stem":"<p>Red light of wavelength 650 nm is incident normally on a diffraction grating with 400 lines per millimetre.</p><p>At what angle to the central maximum is the second-order maximum observed?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["31°","15°","30°","51°","3.0°"],"a":0,"why":"The slit spacing is d = 1.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 400 = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. Then d sin θ = mλ gives sin θ = 2 × 650 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.52, so θ = <strong>31°</strong>. 15° is the first-order angle; 30° doubles the angle, but it is sin θ that is proportional to m; 51° is the third order; 3.0° treats the grating as 400 lines per <em>centimetre</em>."}]},
      {"id":"s1-29","style":"Interference · path difference","stem":"<p>Two loudspeakers, S₁ and S₂, are connected to the same signal generator so that they emit sound waves of frequency 340 Hz in phase. The speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. A microphone is placed at point P.</p><p>What does the microphone detect at P?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two loudspeakers S1 and S2 connected to the same signal generator face right. A straight line labelled 5.0 metres runs from S1 to a point P and another labelled 6.5 metres runs from S2 to the same point P. The figure is not to scale.\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 230\" width=\"420\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"78\" width=\"26\" height=\"54\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e3ecf8\" stroke=\"#222\"/><text x=\"25\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">signal</text><text x=\"25\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">generator</text><polyline points=\"38,92 70,60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"38,118 70,150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polygon points=\"62,48 78,40 78,80 62,72\" fill=\"#e3ecf8\" stroke=\"#222\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"46\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >S₁</text><polygon points=\"62,138 78,130 78,170 62,162\" fill=\"#e3ecf8\" stroke=\"#222\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >S₂</text><line x1=\"78\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"78\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"105\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><text x=\"368\" y=\"109\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >5.0 m</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >6.5 m</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">Not to scale</text></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["A maximum, because the path difference is 1.5 wavelengths","A maximum, because the path difference is greater than one wavelength","A minimum, because the path difference is 0.75 wavelengths","A minimum, because the path difference is 1.5 wavelengths","A maximum, because the two paths are different lengths"],"a":3,"why":"λ = v/f = 340 ÷ 340 = 1.0 m. The path difference is 6.5 − 5.0 = 1.5 m = 1.5λ, which is of the form (m + ½)λ with m = 1. The waves arrive <strong>half a cycle out of phase</strong>, so they interfere destructively and the microphone detects a minimum. A maximum needs a path difference of a whole number of wavelengths (mλ); different path lengths on their own do not guarantee a maximum."}]},
      {"id":"s1-30","style":"Refraction · Snell's law","stem":"<p>A ray of light in air is incident on a glass block of refractive index 1.50. The angle of incidence is 40°.</p><p>What is the angle of refraction in the glass?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["27°","25°","40°","75°","38°"],"a":1,"why":"n = sin θ₁/sin θ₂, so sin θ₂ = sin 40° ÷ 1.50 = 0.429 and θ₂ = <strong>25°</strong> (25° to 2 s.f.) – bending towards the normal as the ray enters the denser glass. 27° divides the <em>angles</em> instead of the sines; 75° multiplies by n instead of dividing; 40° ignores the refraction; 38° is 25° × 1.5, scaling the correct angle by n."}]},
      {"id":"s1-31","style":"Refraction · critical angle","stem":"<p>A ray of light travels inside a glass block of refractive index 1.52 and strikes the glass–air boundary at P with an angle of incidence of 38°, as shown.</p><p>Which statement describes what happens at P?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A ray travelling in glass of refractive index 1.52 strikes a flat glass-air boundary at point P with an angle of incidence of 38 degrees to the normal. A refracted ray in air leaves the boundary at a larger angle, and a faint reflected ray goes back into the glass.\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 260\" width=\"400\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"210\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#e3ecf8\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >glass (n = 1.52)</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >air</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#777\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/><text x=\"358\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">normal</text><line x1=\"111.7983869589917\" y1=\"222.34922129884873\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" /><polygon points=\"168,172 158,180 174,183\" fill=\"#1c3b66\" transform=\"rotate(0)\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"268.7768604059239\" y2=\"27.06140132554995\" stroke=\"#b3261e\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"0\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"174.83924724752944\" y2=\"86.90369672720394\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"168\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >38°</text><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#222\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The ray undergoes total internal reflection","The ray passes straight through into the air without bending","The ray is refracted into the air, bending towards the normal","The ray is partly refracted into the air, bending away from the normal, and partly reflected","The ray is totally absorbed at the boundary"],"a":3,"why":"The critical angle is sin θ<sub>c</sub> = 1/n = 1/1.52, so θ<sub>c</sub> = 41.1°. The angle of incidence (38°) is <strong>less than</strong> the critical angle, so there is no total internal reflection; most light is refracted into the air, bending <em>away</em> from the normal as it speeds up (sin θ = 1.52 × sin 38° gives 69°), and a little is reflected. Bending towards the normal happens when light enters a denser medium; a ray only passes undeviated along the normal."}]},
      {"id":"s1-32","style":"Irradiance · inverse square law","stem":"<p>A small lamp is a point source of light. At a distance of 2.0 m from the lamp the irradiance is 8.0 W m⁻².</p><p>What is the irradiance at a distance of 5.0 m from the lamp?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.3 W m⁻²","3.2 W m⁻²","20 W m⁻²","50 W m⁻²","0.32 W m⁻²"],"a":0,"why":"I₁d₁² = I₂d₂²: 8.0 × 2.0² = I₂ × 5.0², so I₂ = 32 ÷ 25 = <strong>1.28 W m⁻²</strong> (1.3 to 2 s.f.). 3.2 W m⁻² uses the distance ratio without squaring it (8.0 × 2/5); 20 and 50 W m⁻² make the irradiance <em>increase</em> with distance; 0.32 W m⁻² divides 8.0 by 5.0² and ignores the 2.0 m reading."}]},
      {"id":"s1-33","style":"Waves · v = fλ","stem":"<p>A loudspeaker emits sound of frequency 1.7 kHz. The speed of sound in air is 340 m s⁻¹.</p><p>What is the wavelength of this sound in air?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.20 mm","0.20 m","5.0 m","5.8 × 10⁵ m","2.0 × 10² m"],"a":1,"why":"v = fλ so λ = v/f = 340 ÷ 1700 = <strong>0.20 m</strong>. Remember that 1.7 kHz = 1700 Hz. 2.0 × 10² m uses 1.7 as the frequency, forgetting the factor of 1000 in kHz; 5.0 m inverts the equation (f/v); 5.8 × 10⁵ m multiplies 340 by 1700; the 0.20 mm option has the correct digits but the wrong unit."}]},
      {"id":"s1-34","style":"Charges in electric fields · electron gun","stem":"<p>An electron is released from rest at the negative plate of an electron gun and is accelerated across a vacuum by a potential difference of 250 V.</p><p>What is the speed of the electron when it reaches the positive plate?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["6.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹","8.8 × 10¹³ m s⁻¹","9.4 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹","2.3 × 10¹⁶ m s⁻¹","2.2 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"],"a":2,"why":"The work done on the electron is W = QV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 250 = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J, which becomes kinetic energy: ½mv² = QV, so v = √(2QV/m) = √(2 × 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = <strong>9.4 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹</strong>. 6.6 × 10⁶ forgets the 2; 8.8 × 10¹³ leaves out the square root (this is v²); 2.3 × 10¹⁶ leaves out the electron’s charge; 2.2 × 10⁵ uses the proton mass instead of the electron mass."}]},
      {"id":"s1-35","style":"Charges in magnetic fields · particle path","stem":"<p>A beam of electrons enters a region of uniform magnetic field with its velocity at right angles to the field.</p><p>Which statement describes the motion of the electrons in the field?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["They speed up along a straight line, because the magnetic force acts in the direction of motion","They travel along a circular arc and gain speed, because the magnetic force does work on them","They continue in a straight line, because magnetic fields only act on stationary charges","They slow down and stop, because the magnetic force opposes their motion","They travel along a circular arc at constant speed, because the magnetic force is always perpendicular to their velocity"],"a":4,"why":"A magnetic force acts on a <strong>moving</strong> charge at right angles to both the field and the velocity. A force perpendicular to the velocity changes the direction but not the speed (it does no work), so the path is a circular arc in a uniform field. This is the principle used to steer particles round a ring accelerator; the speeding up is done by electric fields."}]},
      {"id":"s1-36","style":"Alternating current · peak and r.m.s.","stem":"<p>The mains supply in the UK is quoted as 230 V.</p><p>What is the peak voltage of the mains supply?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["163 V","325 V","230 V","460 V","650 V"],"a":1,"why":"The quoted 230 V is the r.m.s. value, and V<sub>peak</sub> = √2 × V<sub>rms</sub> = 1.414 × 230 = <strong>325 V</strong>. 163 V divides by √2, which converts the wrong way; 230 V ignores the difference between r.m.s. and peak; 460 V doubles it; 650 V is the peak-to-peak value (2 × 325)."}]},
      {"id":"s1-37","style":"Alternating current · oscilloscope trace","stem":"<p>The trace shows an alternating voltage displayed on an oscilloscope. The timebase is set to 5 ms/div and the Y-gain to 2 V/div.</p><p>What is the r.m.s. voltage of the signal?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Oscilloscope screen with a grid of 10 divisions across and 8 divisions high showing a sine wave centred on the middle line. One complete cycle spans 4 horizontal divisions and the peak is 3 divisions above the centre line. The timebase is 5 milliseconds per division and the Y-gain is 2 volts per division.\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 276\" width=\"340\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"10\" width=\"300\" height=\"240\" fill=\"#f6fbf6\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"260\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#99a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#cdd\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><polyline points=\"20,130 23,115.9 26,102.2 29,89.1 32,77.1 35,66.4 38,57.2 41,49.8 44,44.4 47,41.1 50,40 53,41.1 56,44.4 59,49.8 62,57.2 65,66.4 68,77.1 71,89.1 74,102.2 77,115.9 80,130 83,144.1 86,157.8 89,170.9 92,182.9 95,193.6 98,202.8 101,210.2 104,215.6 107,218.9 110,220 113,218.9 116,215.6 119,210.2 122,202.8 125,193.6 128,182.9 131,170.9 134,157.8 137,144.1 140,130 143,115.9 146,102.2 149,89.1 152,77.1 155,66.4 158,57.2 161,49.8 164,44.4 167,41.1 170,40 173,41.1 176,44.4 179,49.8 182,57.2 185,66.4 188,77.1 191,89.1 194,102.2 197,115.9 200,130 203,144.1 206,157.8 209,170.9 212,182.9 215,193.6 218,202.8 221,210.2 224,215.6 227,218.9 230,220 233,218.9 236,215.6 239,210.2 242,202.8 245,193.6 248,182.9 251,170.9 254,157.8 257,144.1 260,130 263,115.9 266,102.2 269,89.1 272,77.1 275,66.4 278,57.2 281,49.8 284,44.4 287,41.1 290,40 293,41.1 296,44.4 299,49.8 302,57.2 305,66.4 308,77.1 311,89.1 314,102.2 317,115.9 320,130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"170\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >Timebase 5 ms/div        Y-gain 2 V/div</text></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["3.0 V","6.0 V","8.5 V","12 V","4.2 V"],"a":4,"why":"The peak is 3 divisions × 2 V/div = 6.0 V. Then V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>peak</sub>/√2 = 6.0 ÷ 1.414 = <strong>4.2 V</strong> (the frequency, 1/(4 × 5 ms) = 50 Hz, is not needed). 6.0 V is the peak; 8.5 V multiplies by √2 instead of dividing; 12 V is the peak-to-peak value; 3.0 V counts the divisions without using the Y-gain."}]},
      {"id":"s1-38","style":"Circuits · series and parallel resistors","stem":"<p>The battery has negligible internal resistance.</p><p>What is the current in the 6 Ω resistor?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Circuit diagram: a 12 volt battery of negligible internal resistance in series with a 4 ohm resistor, which is in series with a parallel combination of a 12 ohm resistor and a 6 ohm resistor.\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 250\" width=\"400\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><rect x=\"100\" y=\"42\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"122\" y=\"37\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >4 Ω</text><line x1=\"144\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"48\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><line x1=\"54\" y1=\"114\" x2=\"66\" y2=\"114\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"114\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"40\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >12 V</text><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><rect x=\"222\" y=\"95\" width=\"16\" height=\"44\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"246\" y=\"121\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" >12 Ω</text><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"310\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><rect x=\"302\" y=\"95\" width=\"16\" height=\"44\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"324\" y=\"121\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" ></text><text x=\"326\" y=\"121\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" >6 Ω</text><line x1=\"310\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"50\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#222\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"50\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#222\"/><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#222\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#222\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.50 A","1.5 A","2.0 A","1.0 A","0.75 A"],"a":3,"why":"The parallel pair has R = (1/12 + 1/6)⁻¹ = 4.0 Ω, so the total resistance is 4 + 4.0 = 8.0 Ω and the battery current is 12 ÷ 8.0 = 1.5 A. The p.d. across the parallel pair is 1.5 × 4.0 = 6.0 V, so I = V/R = 6.0 ÷ 6 = <strong>1.0 A</strong>. 1.5 A is the total current; 2.0 A is 12 V ÷ 6 Ω, which ignores the 4 Ω resistor; 0.50 A is the current in the 12 Ω resistor; 0.75 A is half the battery current, wrongly assuming the current splits equally."}]},
      {"id":"s1-39","style":"Potential dividers · output voltage","stem":"<p>A 9.0 V supply of negligible internal resistance is connected across a 2.2 kΩ resistor in series with a 4.7 kΩ resistor. A voltmeter of very high resistance is connected across the 4.7 kΩ resistor.</p><p>What is the reading on the voltmeter?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["2.9 V","4.5 V","6.1 V","6.9 V","9.0 V"],"a":2,"why":"V₂ = (R₂/(R₁ + R₂)) × V<sub>S</sub> = (4.7 ÷ 6.9) × 9.0 = <strong>6.1 V</strong>. 2.9 V is the p.d. across the 2.2 kΩ resistor, which is the other share; 4.5 V assumes equal sharing; 6.9 V is the total resistance in kΩ; 9.0 V would only be read if the meter were across both resistors."}]},
      {"id":"s1-40","style":"Potential dividers · thermistor","stem":"<p>A negative-temperature-coefficient thermistor is connected in series with a fixed resistor across a battery. A voltmeter is connected across the <strong>fixed resistor</strong>. The temperature of the thermistor increases.</p><p>Which row describes the change in the thermistor’s resistance and in the voltmeter reading?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The resistance decreases and the reading increases","The resistance decreases and the reading decreases","The resistance increases and the reading increases","The resistance increases and the reading decreases","The resistance and the reading both stay the same"],"a":0,"why":"In an NTC thermistor, resistance <strong>falls</strong> as temperature rises. The series circuit then has a lower thermistor share of the supply, so a larger share of the supply p.d. appears across the fixed resistor (V<sub>fixed</sub> = R<sub>fixed</sub>/(R<sub>fixed</sub> + R<sub>th</sub>) × V<sub>S</sub>) and the voltmeter reading rises. Placing the voltmeter across the thermistor instead would give the opposite change."}]},
      {"id":"s1-41","style":"EMF and internal resistance · terminal p.d.","stem":"<p>A cell of EMF 6.0 V and internal resistance 0.50 Ω is connected to a 2.5 Ω resistor.</p><p>What is the potential difference across the 2.5 Ω resistor?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.0 V","2.0 V","4.8 V","6.0 V","5.0 V"],"a":4,"why":"The current is I = E/(R + r) = 6.0 ÷ (2.5 + 0.50) = 2.0 A, so the p.d. across the resistor (the terminal p.d.) is V = IR = 2.0 × 2.5 = <strong>5.0 V</strong>. 6.0 V is the EMF, which is only the terminal p.d. when no current flows; 1.0 V is the ‘lost volts’, Ir; 2.0 V is the current; 4.8 V ignores the internal resistance when finding the current (6.0 ÷ 2.5 = 2.4 A) and then subtracts the lost volts, 2.4 × 0.50 = 1.2 V, from the EMF."}]},
      {"id":"s1-42","style":"EMF and internal resistance · graph of V against I","stem":"<p>A student measures the terminal potential difference V of a cell for different currents I, and plots the graph shown.</p><p>What is the internal resistance of the cell?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Graph of terminal potential difference V against current I for a cell: a straight line sloping downwards from 1.50 volts at zero current to 1.20 volts at a current of 0.60 amperes, then on to 1.10 volts at 0.80 amperes.\" viewBox=\"0 0 380 270\" width=\"380\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"16\" width=\"304\" height=\"206\" fill=\"#fbfcfe\" stroke=\"#ccd\"/><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"58\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0.0</text><line x1=\"134\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"134\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"134\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0.2</text><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0.4</text><line x1=\"286\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"286\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"286\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"286\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"286\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0.6</text><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"362\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0.8</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0.0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"170.5\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"170.5\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"170.5\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"170.5\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"174.5\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0.5</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"119\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"119\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"119\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"119\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >1.0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"67.5\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"67.5\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"67.5\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"67.5\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"71.5\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >1.5</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >2.0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >I / A</text><text transform=\"translate(14 119) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\">V / V</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"67.5\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"108.7\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" /><circle cx=\"58\" cy=\"67.5\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><circle cx=\"286\" cy=\"98.4\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.30 Ω","1.5 Ω","0.50 Ω","2.0 Ω","1.2 Ω"],"a":2,"why":"V = E − Ir, so the line has intercept E (1.50 V) and <strong>gradient −r</strong>. The gradient is (1.20 − 1.50) ÷ (0.60 − 0) = −0.50 V A⁻¹, so r = <strong>0.50 Ω</strong>. 1.5 is the EMF read off the axis; 2.0 Ω is V ÷ I for the point at 0.60 A, the resistance of the <em>external</em> circuit; 0.30 Ω is the fall in terminal p.d. rather than the gradient; 1.2 is a voltage reading."}]},
      {"id":"s1-43","style":"Capacitors · energy stored","stem":"<p>A capacitor of capacitance 470 µF is charged until the potential difference across it is 12 V.</p><p>How much energy is stored in the capacitor?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["6.8 × 10⁻² J","3.4 × 10⁻² J","5.6 × 10⁻³ J","3.4 × 10⁴ J","2.8 × 10⁻³ J"],"a":1,"why":"E = ½CV² = ½ × 470 × 10⁻⁶ × 12² = <strong>3.4 × 10⁻² J</strong>. 6.8 × 10⁻² J leaves out the ½; 5.6 × 10⁻³ J is the <em>charge</em> in coulombs (Q = CV); 3.4 × 10⁴ J forgets to convert µF to F; 2.8 × 10⁻³ J forgets to square the voltage."}]},
      {"id":"s1-44","style":"Capacitors · charging through a resistor","stem":"<p>A capacitor charges through a resistor from a 9.0 V supply of negligible internal resistance. The graph shows how the charging current varies with time.</p><p>What is the resistance of the resistor?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Graph of current against time for a capacitor charging through a resistor: the current starts at 1.5 milliamperes at time zero and falls in a curve that flattens out, reaching almost zero by 10 seconds.\" viewBox=\"0 0 380 270\" width=\"380\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"16\" width=\"304\" height=\"206\" fill=\"#fbfcfe\" stroke=\"#ccd\"/><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"58\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >0</text><line x1=\"118.8\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"118.8\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"118.8\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"118.8\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"118.8\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >2</text><line x1=\"179.6\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"179.6\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"179.6\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"179.6\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"179.6\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >4</text><line x1=\"240.4\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"240.4\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"240.4\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"240.4\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"240.4\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >6</text><line x1=\"301.2\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"301.2\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"301.2\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"301.2\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"301.2\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >8</text><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"362\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"227\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"362\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >10</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0.0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"153.3\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"153.3\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"153.3\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"153.3\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"157.3\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >0.5</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"84.7\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"84.7\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"84.7\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"84.7\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"88.7\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >1.0</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#dde\" stroke-width=\"1\" /><line x1=\"53\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" >1.5</text><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"58\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><text x=\"210\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" >t / s</text><text transform=\"translate(14 119) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\">I / mA</text><polyline points=\"58,16 65.6,35.6 73.2,53.3 80.8,69.4 88.4,83.9 96,97.1 103.6,108.9 111.2,119.7 118.8,129.4 126.4,138.2 134,146.2 141.6,153.4 149.2,160 156.8,165.9 164.4,171.2 172,176 179.6,180.4 187.2,184.4 194.8,187.9 202.4,191.2 210,194.1 217.6,196.8 225.2,199.2 232.8,201.3 240.4,203.3 248,205.1 255.6,206.7 263.2,208.2 270.8,209.5 278.4,210.7 286,211.7 293.6,212.7 301.2,213.6 308.8,214.4 316.4,215.1 324,215.8 331.6,216.4 339.2,216.9 346.8,217.4 354.4,217.8 362,218.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1c3b66\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["0.17 kΩ","3.0 kΩ","12 kΩ","6.0 kΩ","14 kΩ"],"a":3,"why":"At the instant charging starts the capacitor has no p.d., so the whole 9.0 V is across the resistor: R = V/I = 9.0 ÷ 1.5 × 10⁻³ = 6000 Ω = <strong>6.0 kΩ</strong>. The current falls as the capacitor charges because its p.d. opposes the supply, so the resistance must be found from the starting current. 12 kΩ halves the starting current (9.0 ÷ 0.75 mA); 14 kΩ is V × I with the current left in mA; 0.17 kΩ is I ÷ V with the current left in mA; 3.0 kΩ doubles the initial current."}]},
      {"id":"s1-45","style":"Semiconductors · doping","stem":"<p>A pure silicon crystal is doped with a small number of phosphorus atoms. Each phosphorus atom has five outer electrons; silicon has four.</p><p>Which statement describes the doped material?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["It is n-type: the extra electrons from phosphorus are free to move and carry current","It is n-type: the phosphorus atoms leave holes that carry current","It is p-type: the extra electrons from phosphorus are free to move and carry current","It is p-type: the phosphorus atoms leave holes that carry current","It is an insulator: the extra electrons fill all the bonds in the crystal"],"a":0,"why":"Four of the phosphorus atom’s outer electrons make bonds with neighbouring silicon atoms; the <strong>fifth is free</strong> to move through the crystal. The majority charge carriers are negative electrons, so the material is <strong>n</strong>-type. Holes are the majority carriers in p-type material, made by doping with an element such as boron that has only three outer electrons. The doped material conducts better than pure silicon."}]},
      {"id":"s1-46","style":"p–n junctions · bias","stem":"<p>A p–n junction is connected to a cell as shown.</p><p>Which row describes the junction in this circuit?</p>","figure":"<svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A p-n junction diode drawn as a p-type block on the left and an n-type block on the right with a narrow depletion layer between them. A cell is connected across the diode with its negative terminal (short plate) joined to the p-type side and its positive terminal (long plate) joined to the n-type side.\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 250\" width=\"400\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><rect x=\"60\" y=\"30\" width=\"110\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#f6dede\" stroke=\"#222\"/><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"30\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#f1f1f1\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"30\" width=\"110\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#dee8f6\" stroke=\"#222\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\">p</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\">n</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">junction</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"310\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"166\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"168\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"192\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><line x1=\"166\" y1=\"152\" x2=\"166\" y2=\"208\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"3\" /><text x=\"138\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">−</text><text x=\"242\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+</text><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" /><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"0\" /><text x=\"190\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#222\" font-size=\"11\">cell</text></svg>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["Reverse biased; the depletion layer widens and almost no current flows","Forward biased; the depletion layer narrows and a current flows","Reverse biased; the depletion layer narrows and a current flows","Forward biased; the depletion layer widens and almost no current flows","Reverse biased; the depletion layer is unchanged and a large current flows"],"a":0,"why":"The positive terminal is joined to the n-type side and the negative terminal to the p-type side, so the cell pulls electrons and holes <strong>away</strong> from the junction: it is <strong>reverse biased</strong>. The depletion layer widens, which stops the carriers crossing, so almost no current flows. In forward bias (positive to p-type) the depletion layer narrows and carriers cross the junction, giving a current."}]},
      {"id":"s1-47","style":"Semiconductors · LED","stem":"<p>A light-emitting diode (LED) is connected in a circuit so that it is forward biased and emits light.</p><p>Which statement explains how the light is produced?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["Electrons are removed from the junction, producing light as they leave the material","Photons from the cell are reflected at the junction","The depletion layer widens and the energy stored in it is released as light","Electrons cross the junction and fall into holes, releasing the energy as photons","Holes cross the junction and fall into electrons in the p-type region, releasing no energy"],"a":3,"why":"In forward bias, electrons from the n-type side and holes from the p-type side meet at the junction. Each time an electron <strong>recombines with a hole</strong> it drops from the conduction band to a lower energy level, and the energy difference is released as a photon (E = hf); the band gap sets the colour. A widening depletion layer is the reverse-bias case, which does not emit light."}]},
      {"id":"s1-48","style":"Semiconductors · solar cell","stem":"<p>A solar cell is a p–n junction that is not connected to any external supply.</p><p>Which statement describes how a solar cell works?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The cell is forward biased by the Sun’s heat so that a current is driven round the circuit","Photons absorbed near the junction produce electron–hole pairs, which the junction separates to produce an EMF","Photons from the light heat the junction, which expands and pushes charge round the circuit","The depletion layer is removed by the light, so the cell becomes a conductor","Photons are reflected by the junction, pushing electrons into the p-type side"],"a":1,"why":"Photons with enough energy lift electrons across the band gap near the junction, producing <strong>electron–hole pairs</strong>. The electric field of the junction pushes the electrons towards the n-type side and holes to the p-type side, setting up a potential difference that drives a current through an external circuit. No external bias is applied, which is why a solar cell works as a source, not a load."}]},
      {"id":"s1-49","style":"Power · P = V²/R","stem":"<p>A resistor connected across a 6.0 V supply dissipates 3.0 W. The supply voltage is increased to 12 V. The resistance of the resistor stays constant.</p><p>What is the power dissipated in the resistor at 12 V?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1.5 W","3.0 W","6.0 W","24 W","12 W"],"a":4,"why":"For a fixed resistance P = V²/R, so doubling the voltage multiplies the power by 2² = 4: P = 4 × 3.0 = <strong>12 W</strong>. 6.0 W and 24 W treat the power as proportional to V or to V³-style scaling; 3.0 W ignores the change; 1.5 W has the power falling when the voltage rises."}]},
      {"id":"s1-50","style":"Uncertainties · random uncertainty","stem":"<p>A student measures the time for a trolley to travel down a slope five times and records 3.40 s, 3.50 s, 3.44 s, 3.46 s and 3.45 s.</p><p>What is the approximate random uncertainty in the mean time?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["± 0.10 s","± 0.05 s","± 0.02 s","± 0.03 s","± 0.2 s"],"a":2,"why":"random uncertainty = (maximum value − minimum value) ÷ number of values = (3.50 − 3.40) ÷ 5 = <strong>0.02 s</strong>. 0.10 s is the full range; 0.05 s divides by 2 and 0.03 s by 3 rather than by the 5 readings; 0.2 s doubles the range. The mean is 3.45 s, so the result is quoted as (3.45 ± 0.02) s."}]},
      {"id":"s1-51","style":"Uncertainties · percentage uncertainty","stem":"<p>A student records four measurements, each with its absolute uncertainty:</p><p>(0.500 ± 0.005) kg;&nbsp; (0.80 ± 0.01) m;&nbsp; (2.0 ± 0.1) s;&nbsp; (6.0 ± 0.2) V.</p><p>Which measurement has the greatest <strong>percentage</strong> uncertainty?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["The time, (2.0 ± 0.1) s","The mass, (0.500 ± 0.005) kg","The length, (0.80 ± 0.01) m","The potential difference, (6.0 ± 0.2) V","They all have the same percentage uncertainty"],"a":0,"why":"Percentage uncertainty = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measurement) × 100: mass 1.0 %, length 1.3 %, time <strong>5.0 %</strong>, potential difference 3.3 %. The time has the largest <em>percentage</em> uncertainty even though the potential difference has the largest absolute uncertainty (0.2). The measurement with the greatest percentage uncertainty is the one that most limits the precision of a final result."}]},
      {"id":"s1-52","style":"Practical skills · significant figures","stem":"<p>A trolley has a mass of 0.45 kg and a speed of 3.20 m s⁻¹. A student calculates its momentum on a calculator, which displays 1.44.</p><p>How should the student quote the momentum?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["1 kg m s⁻¹","1.44 kg m s⁻¹","1.440 kg m s⁻¹","1.4 kg m s⁻¹","1.5 kg m s⁻¹"],"a":3,"why":"The final answer should be given to the same number of significant figures as the <strong>least precise</strong> piece of data. 0.45 kg has two significant figures (3.20 has three), so p = mv = 1.4 kg m s⁻¹. Quoting 1.44 or 1.440 claims a precision the mass measurement does not have; 1 kg m s⁻¹ throws away a figure unnecessarily; 1.5 rounds 1.44 the wrong way."}]},
      {"id":"s1-53","style":"Practical skills · graphs and the inverse square law","stem":"<p>A student investigates how the irradiance <em>I</em> of light from a small lamp varies with the distance <em>d</em> from the lamp, in order to verify the inverse square law, <em>I</em> = <em>k</em>/<em>d</em>².</p><p>Which graph should give a straight line through the origin?</p>","parts":[{"type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Select one answer.","opts":["I against d","I against 1/d²","I against d²","I against 1/d","d against I"],"a":1,"why":"Since <em>I</em> = <em>k</em> × (1/<em>d</em>²), a graph of <em>I</em> against 1/<em>d</em>² is a straight line through the origin with gradient <em>k</em>. A graph of <em>I</em> against <em>d</em> is a curve, as is <em>I</em> against <em>d</em>²; <em>I</em> against 1/<em>d</em> curves because the irradiance is proportional to 1/<em>d</em>², not 1/<em>d</em>; and <em>d</em> against <em>I</em> is just the original curve with its axes swapped."}]},
    ],
  };
})();
